{
  "data": {
    "text": "Flexible Connectivity in the Aging Brain\nRevealed by Task Modulations\n\nLinda Geerligs, Emi Saliasi, Remco J. Renken, Natasha M. Maurits, and Monicque M. Lorist\nHuman Brain Mapping 35:3788–3804 (2014). DOI 10.1002/hbm.22437.\n\nParticipants\n\nForty younger (21 males, Mage = 20.6 years, range: 18–26 years) and 40 older adults (24 males, Mage = 64.9 years, range: 59–74 years) participated in the experiment after giving informed consent. All participants were right handed and had no history of neurological or psychiatric disorders. They had normal or corrected-to-normal visual acuity. All participants scored 26 or higher on the Mini Mental State Examination (MMSE) and below 16 on both subscales of the Hospital Anxiety and Depression Scale (HADS). The study adhered to the Declaration of Helsinki and was approved by the local ethics committee of the University Medical Center Groningen, the Netherlands. Data of one young and one older participant were lost due to technical problems. One older participant was excluded because a brain abnormality was detected.\n\nBehavioral Data (exclusions)\n\nData of three younger participants and one older participant were excluded because their accuracy on the SA task was around or below chance level (below 60%) in one or more task conditions. For the n-back task, all participants performed the 0- and 1-back task with over 70% accuracy… Thus, the behavioral and ICA analyses were performed on the data of 36 younger and 37 older participants.\n\nFNC sample sizes\n\nThose participants (four younger and two older) for which fewer than 150 scans remained in one or more conditions due to the movement correction procedure were excluded from the FNC analysis. Thus, the FNC analysis was performed on the data of 32 younger and 35 older participants.\n\nImage Acquisition\n\nFMRI scans were obtained with a 3 Tesla MR scanner (3T Achieva, Philips Medical Systems, Best, Netherlands), with echo planar imaging (EPI) capability and an eight channel SENSE head coil. Functional images were obtained with the following pulse sequence parameter settings: single shot EPI; 37 slices; slice thickness = 3.5 mm; no gap; field of view = 224 mm; matrix scan size 64 by 64; transverse slice orientation; repetition time (TR) = 2000 msec; echo time (TE) = 30 msec; minimal temporal slice timing = 1836 msec; flip angle 70°. A 3-D T1-weighted anatomical scan of the entire brain was obtained for each participant using the following pulse sequence parameters: field of view = 256 mm; matrix scan size 256 by 256; 170 slices; slice thickness 1 mm; transverse slice orientation; TE = 3.6 msec; TR = 9 msec; flip angle 8°.\n\nProcedure and Cognitive Tasks (overview)\n\nfMRI data was recorded in three different runs, in which participants performed an n-back task, a selective attention (SA) task and 10-min of resting state. During resting state, participants were instructed to keep their eyes closed, but not to fall asleep.\n\nN-Back Task\n\nThe n-back task had three load conditions; 0-, 1-, and 2-back. Each block started with the presentation of task instructions. Subsequently, in each trial participants were presented with a single stimulus letter (500 msec). Each letter was randomly positioned in one of eight possible locations. The inter trial interval varied randomly between 1,000 and 2,000 msec. Each load condition was presented twice, resulting in a total of six task blocks, with 100 trials each. In each block, targets occurred randomly in 50% of the trials. Blocks were followed by a 30 sec fixation cross.\n\nSelective Attention Task\n\nSix experimental blocks, each containing 63 trials were presented. In each trial, the stimuli, consisting of four letters positioned at the end points of both diagonals, were presented for 300 msec followed by an interstimulus interval varying randomly between 2,000 and 2,500 msec. Participants were required to press the “yes” button when the target letter was presented on a relevant diagonal position [target]… Relevant target trials made up 33% of the total number of trials. Each block was followed by a 30 sec fixation cross.\n\nfMRI Data Analysis (preprocessing)\n\nOffline processing was performed using SPM 8. The functional images were motion-corrected and coregistered to the anatomical scan. Bias regularization (SPM 8) was used to reduce signal intensity variations due to field inhomogeneities in both structural and functional images. A group specific anatomic template was created using DARTEL, to optimize inter-participant alignment. Data were smoothed with an 8 mm full-width half maximum (FWHM) Gaussian kernel.\n\nFunctional Connectivity Analysis (cleanup)\n\nThe default procedure in GIFT is to detrend the linear, quadratic and cubic trends in the time-courses. Subsequently, residual effects of motion were corrected by regression with the six realignment parameters and their temporal derivatives. In addition, variance associated with stimulus presentation was removed in the SA-task and the n-back task, to make sure that connectivity is not dominated by synchronized stimulus-evoked responses. In both tasks, we convolved all regressors with the canonical hemodynamic response function (HRF), as well as the temporal derivative and the dispersion derivative to account for local variability in the shape of the HRF.\n\nMovement correction (scrubbing)\n\nThe total displacement per scan was computed… Scans in which the displacement compared with the previous scan was larger than .5 mm were flagged. The flagged scans as well as two scans before and one scan after this scan were not taken into account in the computation of the correlation matrix. We used part of the procedure applied by Power et al. (2012) and the ArtRepair toolbox.\n\nSoftware and toolboxes\n\nE-prime 1.2 was used for stimulus generation and response collection. Offline processing was performed using SPM 8. Data were decomposed using the GIFT toolbox (version 2.0e) and ICASSO; DARTEL was used for template creation; SPSS was used for statistics; the ArtRepair toolbox was used for motion-related procedures."
  },
